CAGE AGAINST THE MACHINE 2: RECEIPT CHECK

Black screen.

We hear the soft squeak of a shopping cart wheel that desperately needs maintenance.

Then—

BEEP.

The Costco receipt checker looks Nicolas Cage dead in the eyes.

“Receipt.”

Cage slowly turns around.

Tiny Cage is still sitting on his shoulder eating a sample of chicken sausage like he pays taxes.

Jessica whispers, “Just give him the receipt.”

Cage: “I can’t.”

Jessica: “Why?”

Cage leans in.

“Because this receipt… hasn’t happened yet.”

Tiny Cage immediately spits sausage.

“OH, FUCK OFF.”

The checker sighs.

“Sir, I just need to mark it with the highlighter.”

Cage looks down.

Yellow highlighter.

His pupils dilate.

“Yellow.”

Jessica: “Yes, Nicolas. Yellow.”

“No. Gold.”

He rips open his jacket.

Inside: maps. Polaroids. Three Costco membership cards. A compass. One loose mozzarella stick.

He slams an ancient parchment onto a shopping cart.

“Benjamin Franklin invented the highlighter.”

Jessica: “No he didn’t.”

Cage: “THAT’S WHAT THEY WANT YOU TO THINK.”

Tiny Cage: “He’s cooking. Let him cook.”

The receipt checker has already mentally left his body.

Then Cage notices something.

Every receipt leaving Costco has the same yellow slash.

Except…

When viewed together from above…

The slashes form a map.

Cage whispers:

“Oh my God.”

Jessica: “What?”

Cage grabs a nearby elderly man’s receipt.

“Sir, national emergency.”

The old man goes, “The fuck?”

Cage starts collecting receipts from everyone exiting.

Now he’s crawling around the Costco entrance arranging them on the floor like a deranged coupon archaeologist.

People are stepping over him.

A kid drops a churro on the map.

Cage freezes.

Slowly looks at it.

“…the churro is the key.”

Tiny Cage: “Brother, you cannot keep saying that every time food lands somewhere.”

Cage: “WATCH ME.”

He rotates the churro 17 degrees.

The receipts align perfectly.

They point to one location:

WASHINGTON, D.C.

Jessica stares.

“Are we stealing the Declaration of Independence again?”

Cage looks deeply offended.

“No.”

Beat.

“We’re returning it.”

⸻

Six hours later.

Cage is driving a rented Dodge Caravan toward Washington at 104 miles per hour.

Jessica: “Why did you rent a minivan?”

Cage: “Nobody suspects the Caravan.”

Tiny Cage, standing on the dashboard with sunglasses on:

“TACTICAL SOCCER MOM.”

Cage:

“Exactly.”

Jessica:

“There are four police cars behind us.”

Cage checks mirror.

“…They suspect the Caravan.”

Hard cut.

The minivan is now somehow inside the Smithsonian.

Nobody explains how.

Cage steps out.

Alarm immediately starts screaming.

Cage screams back.

“YES, I HEAR YOU.”

They move through the museum.

Cage suddenly stops in front of George Washington’s portrait.

Washington is pointing.

Cage points back.

Jessica: “What are you doing?”

“He started it.”

Then Tiny Cage notices the painting’s eyes are aimed slightly downward.

They follow the gaze.

There’s a plaque.

PLEASE DO NOT TOUCH.

Cage touches it.

Entire wall opens.

Jessica:

“You motherfucker.”

Cage smiles.

“That sign wasn’t a warning.”

Tiny Cage:

“It was a tutorial.”

Behind the wall is a staircase descending underground.

They reach a chamber filled with historical artifacts nobody has ever seen.

Napoleon’s AirPods.

Lincoln’s vape.

A medieval parking ticket.

One perfect unopened Blockbuster VHS copy of Con Air.

Cage sees it.

Stops breathing.

Jessica sees the cover.

Looks at Cage.

Looks back at the cover.

“…Why are you on this?”

Cage, trembling:

“I don’t know.”

Tiny Cage takes off his sunglasses.

“Jesus Christ.”

Regular Cage picks up the VHS.

On the back is handwritten text:

NICOLAS—IF YOU’RE READING THIS, THE LOOP HAS BEGUN.

Cage screams for eleven straight seconds.

Not scared.

Just Nicolas Cage.

Then the television in the chamber flickers on.

Static.

A silhouette appears.

It’s another Nicolas Cage.

Older.

Eye patch.

Mustache.

Absolutely no explanation.

Future Cage says:

“Listen carefully. You have 72 hours.”

Current Cage:

“Until what?”

Future Cage:

“Until Nicolas Cage becomes public domain.”

Jessica:

“That’s not how that works.”

Future Cage:

“THERE’S NO TIME.”

Tiny Cage:

“ASK HIM WHY I’M SMALL.”

Current Cage:

“WHY IS HE SMALL?”

Future Cage:

“…He knows what he did.”

Tiny Cage:

“FUCK.”

Transmission cuts.

Silence.

Then somewhere deep beneath Washington, D.C., machinery awakens.

A giant red countdown begins.

71:59:59

Jessica:

“So what now?”

Cage slowly puts on his sunglasses.

“We find out who owns Nicolas Cage.”

Tiny Cage climbs into his breast pocket.

“I call shotgun.”

Jessica:

“You are literally eight inches tall.”

Tiny Cage:

“AND YET I HAVE CALLED IT.”

Cage kicks open the emergency exit.

They walk dramatically into daylight.

Immediately get hit by a Segway tour.

CUT TO BLACK.

CAGE AGAINST THE MACHINE 3: INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY

Some men steal treasure.

Nicolas Cage steals the rights to himself.